🛡️ Credit Card Fraud Detection with Machine Learning

This project was completed as part of my Data Analytics internship at Oasis Infobyte. The goal was to build a robust fraud detection model capable of identifying fraudulent credit card transactions despite severe class imbalance.

🔍 The Problem:

Fraud detection datasets are notoriously imbalanced. In this dataset of 10,000 transactions, only 1.51% are fraudulent. Traditional models trained on imbalanced data tend to be biased toward the majority class, missing most frauds.

🛠️ Approach:

1. Exploratory Data Analysis — Understanding transaction patterns, amounts, time of day, and risk indicators
2. Feature Engineering — Identifying key predictors of fraud
3. Class Imbalance Handling — Applied SMOTE (Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique) to balance the training data
4. Model Building — Trained and compared:
   · Logistic Regression (with class_weight='balanced')
   · Random Forest (with SMOTE)
5. Evaluation — Used Recall, Precision, F1 Score, and AUC-ROC (not accuracy!)

📊 Key Findings:

Feature Insight Business Implication
Fraudulent transactions tend to have higher amounts Flag high-value transactions for review
Device trust score is lower for frauds Use device fingerprinting
Location mismatch is a strong indicator Implement geographic verification
Velocity (transactions in 24h) matters Monitor rapid transaction patterns

📈 Model Performance:

Model Recall Precision F1 Score AUC-ROC
Logistic Regression 1.00 0.073 0.136 0.972
Random Forest 0.644 0.192 0.296 0.939

⚠️ Critical Lesson: Why Accuracy is Misleading

In imbalanced datasets, accuracy is a dangerous metric. A model predicting "not fraud" for everything would achieve 98.49% accuracy but catch zero frauds. In fraud detection, we prioritize:

· Recall — Don't miss fraud!
· Precision — Minimize false alarms
· AUC-ROC — Overall class separation ability

